WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
And I'm here representing United We Dream
as a Southwest regional core leader.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.000
Um, I think my work really started
primarily after seeing the fear

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.020
surrounding the L.A. raids in the summer
of 2025.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:16.020
And through Project Second Voice, I've
been sharing immigrant stories, connecting

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:21.120
people with resources, and turning
awareness into advocacy, and sharing how

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:27.060
each individual impacted by legislation,
and showed that their fathers, their sons,

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.640
their family, daughters, mothers, right?

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.880
They're not just people that you don't
know, and they're people that really make

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.860
an impact in our community and really, um,
should have a spot in America, right?

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:43.800
So, through this organization, Michelle
Medina contacted me and asked

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.360
her to share, uh, her story, and tonight I
want you to hear her words and understand

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.940
the future she is fighting for alongside
all of us.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:56.760
So, Michelle grew up in San Antonio,
Texas, and, um, basically, I'll just tell

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.940
her, I'll just tell you exactly kind of
what she told me, right?

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.260
So, I came to the United States when I was
four years old, and this is the only home

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.280
I had ever known. I attended elementary
school, middle school, and high school

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.060
here, and I later earned my bachelor's
degree throughout my life.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.060
I've worked hard to give back to my
community and contribute to the city that

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:19.020
raised me. And in high school, Michelle
also founded the Mexican American society,

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.140
so Latino students could have
representation in our community.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.940
She earned her bachelor's degree in
criminal justice, and hoped to pursue a

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:31.620
career in criminal law, to help create a
fair, more inclusive America for her

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:36.540
community. And she was preparing to serve
the community that really raised her and

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.960
made, it gave her the opportunity she has.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:44.100
But Michelle described being unable to
access DACA, even though she was eligible

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:49.640
due to recent pressures against DACA for
new applicants from the government.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:55.560
And this led to her and many others, to
have no protection and benefits from DACA.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:00.240
And some of you on this call might
experience this herself, and you, too, may

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.320
have been part of the tens of thousands of
immigrants, and young people who have been

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.200
blocked out of the DACA program or know
someone who has.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.480
And Michelle was later detained by ICE and
spent 25 days in detention before,

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.100
thankfully, being released on bond because
of community organization efforts that

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.960
fought for freedom. And she described
being in detention as one of the most

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.700
difficult experiences of her life.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:28.200
And thinking about the distance between
the future she was working towards and the

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.800
uncertainty, uncertainty she suddenly
faced.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:35.100
Uh, I think this is what, really, people
should understand, and what she wants

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:40.140
people to understand, is that, uh, she's
not asking for special treatment, and she

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:44.100
is simply an undocumented person with
dreams, hopes, and goals like everybody

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:49.140
else. She just wants the opportunity to
continue building the future that was laid

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:53.760
down in front of her, serving her own
community, and gave it back to the city

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.980
that really raised her up. And after her
release, she faced a mountain of legal

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:04.800
expenses and fees, uh, and because of the
fact that she lost her work permit,

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.820
she was really struggling with this,
because she couldn't have authorization to

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:14.460
work. And Michelle explains that she's
responsible for attorney fees,

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:18.240
immigration filing fees, and the many
costs that come with fighting for the

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.560
chance to remain in the only country she
has ever called home.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.860
And she completed college, and wanted to
use her education, but during this time,

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:29.820
she couldn't because of detention, and all
the stuff that has happened to her.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:34.560
And what Michelle's story really should
remind us is that access to citizenship

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:39.180
isn't the same as safety, and it's about
financial stability.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.920
It's about delivering for working people.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:45.120
And it's not just about the citizenship
that matters, right?

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.780
And at a time when working people all
across the country are struggling to pay

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:53.280
for groceries, rent, childcare,
healthcare, you name it.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.840
Michelle, too, faces this on top of having
no steady income, no work authorization

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.000
and no clear pathway to citizenship.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.320
I feel like uncertainty reaches into
everybody's decisions in everyday

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.120
decisions. Pursuing career.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.360
You're always thinking in the back of my
mind, I don't have legal status, what

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.680
might happen if I'm on the street or
whatever, right?

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.400
Uh, planning financially, and imagining a
future near loved ones.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.260
Uncertainty is always in the back of your
mind.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:21.920
And families share the way of those
unanswered questions.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:26.220
Michelle has expressed what she wanted for
her life, and that she is not just an

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:30.480
immigration case or a statistic. She is a
working class person in this country that

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.880
deserves an equal chance, just like
anybody else.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:34.640
Uh, quoting for Michelle.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:39.240
She said that, like so many others, I want
the opportunity to work hard, build a

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.900
career, support my family, and make a
positive contribution to the country I've

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:47.460
called home. I spent years living with
uncertainty, but I continue to hold onto

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:52.440
the hope that one day, I will be able to
pursue my dreams without fear, and with

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.440
the same opportunities that others are
given.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.340
Her dreams, her hopes, are no different
than the millions of working people in

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.840
this country who are demanding the same
out of the future, which really push,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.880
really emphasizes really the need to push
for a permanent pathway, uh, to

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.380
citizenship. Through Project Second Boys,
I helped share Michelle's story and

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:16.880
fundraiser, and people shared, donated,
and offered support.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:21.240
And Michelle's told me that donations has
really helped her pay off her first

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:25.560
immigration case, and also cover her work
permit related expenses in obtaining her

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:29.360
license, which is a huge win for her in
the past few weeks.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.480
And since she has obtained work
authorization and the driver's license,

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.780
she has shared that she has actually
started her first job with the work

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:40.620
authorization, and she's actually driving
again and really gaining back her

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:44.940
independence that was really taken away
from her, uh, those past months ago.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:48.420
And these moments really show what
rebuilding a life looks like.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:52.980
What the power of organizing is capable
of, and the lives that can be changed as

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:55.860
we work together as a community, and
refuse to give up.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:59.700
But Michelle is still navigating to
immigration cases, and her story really

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:05.280
isn't over, and the progress she's made
matters enormously, yet she is still

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:08.880
waiting for the lasting security that she
still hasn't got it.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:13.580
Right now, our community is on the cusp of
moving the Dream and Promise Act forward.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:17.520
A bill that would ensure citizenship for
five million people, including Michelle.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:21.960
And a discharge position, uh, petition to
move that legislation forward is one

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.540
signature short, from forcing a House
vote.

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:28.800
only one signature is needed. I want to be
clear about something, but we cannot

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.540
control whether or not Congress does the
right thing, or if one more member of the

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:38.520
House signs this discharge petition, We
have already sent an unequivocal message.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:44.400
We have secured 217 signatures under, I
would say,

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:48.420
horrible conditions, and at a time, when
deadly raids have caused havoc in our

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.840
cities, when neighbors and loved ones
having killed in our streets, and when

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:55.680
billions of dollars have been approved to
fuel more immigration enforcement and

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.420
attentions. Despite all of this, we have
organized, and we have certainly done what

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:04.320
many thought we couldn't have achieved.
And this is what we must remember as we

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:07.320
continue to push for this final 218th
signature.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:12.840
No matter what, we have set an undeniable
precedent, principle of how we

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:15.440
expect Dream and Promise to be
prioritized.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:19.680
And certainly, that message will be known
on day one for the new Congress taking

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:24.480
office in January. So why do these stories
matter, and why should you continue

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:29.460
sharing them? For DACA recipients,
immigrant youth, and TPS holders, lasting

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:33.660
protection means the security to use an
education, build a career, support loved

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:38.400
ones, and plan a future. Knowing Michelle
has made me more aware of the lives behind

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:43.440
every policy discussion, and her story
helps people understand her circumstances

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:47.220
and respond with support and really
understand what is really happening in our

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.680
government, which they might have not
really known before.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:54.360
Tonight, we have the opportunities to
uplift stories like Michelle's and push

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:57.900
our congressional targets to get to the
final 218th signature.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:03.060
Stories help representatives see how their
decisions really affect someone, and not

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:09.000
make them just another name in a bunch of
emails that they get, and also a name on

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.220
a paper that they might not even see,
right?

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:14.160
Names show that it's someone that is being
affected.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.760
Someone's education, work, family, and
future.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.180
And they give us a way to explain why we
care and why these actions matter.

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:25.020
Michelle trusted me with her experiences,
and carrying her story means listening

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:29.160
carefully. Sharing it honestly and acting
on what we have already heard.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:33.540
And when we call her representatives, we
can carry her words with us, we can speak

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:37.920
about people in our communities, too, and
make clear why permanent protection

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:42.540
matters to us. I keep returning to
Michelle's hope that one day, she can

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:46.920
pursue her dreams without fear. For
someone who had arrived at 4 and built her

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:50.280
life here on the one day, has already met
years of waiting.

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:54.900
She deserves the chance to look ahead with
confidence, to pursue her career, support

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:58.080
her family, and build a secure future in
the place she calls home.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:01.560
Michelle, thank you for trusting me with
your story.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:06.060
He's not here today, but, uh, thank you
for everyone for listening and preparing

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:09.360
to act. And as Ayah leads us into the call
and action.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:12.240
Please participate. Follow the organizer's
instructions.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:16.560
Contact [unclear] and urge them to support
the Dream and Promise Act and tell them

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:21.660
why it matters to you. And then let's turn
what we have earned into action, and keep

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:26.100
showing up after tonight. Michelle's home
is here, and she deserves the security

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:28.040
to build her future here, too. Thank you.
